# <%= current_page.data.title %>

Screen reader users need to be informed of status updates.

## What WCAG says:

> In content implemented using markup languages, status messages can be programmatically determined through role or properties such that they can be presented to the user by assistive technologies without receiving focus.

Check the official guidance for [Understanding 4.1.3 Status Messages](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/status-messages)

## What this means

Changes to content that take place without reloading the page, and which require a user's attention, are announced without user intervention and without moving keyboard focus.

Examples of relevant changes include live notifications, client-side error messages, or loading notifications that update in real time.

Showing and hiding any other content on the page is not covered by this criterion. For example, the contents of an expanded menu, or the appearance of a new item in a section of scrollable content do not need to be announced when they appear.

## Why it matters

Screen reader users may be unable to tell when a status message has been dynamically updated if they are not focused on the element being updated.

## How to check

Using a screen reader, activate a feature that dynamically changes part of a page's content.

Any status message should be announced without needing to move the keyboard focus to the element containing the announced text.

### For important status changes

If the user should be informed of the status as soon as possible, such as something that prevents further interaction, the status message should be read immediately, interrupting any text currently being announced.

There may be situations where it's helpful to programmatically move keyboard focus to the element being announced, such as error messages or dialogs that require user intervention to dismiss.

### For passive status changes

If the user doesn't need to be informed immediately, such as if a task completed successfully or a live feed has been updated, the text currently being announced should finish being read first, followed by the status message. The user's keyboard or screen reader focus should not move to the status element.

### How to check status messages in code

To determine whether a status message is going to be read out to screen reader users through the code, inspect the message in the browser's inspector.

Adding the following attributes to an element will cause anything inside it to be read to screen reader users:

* `aria-live="assertive"` - this announces an important status change as described earlier.
* `aria-live="polite"` - this announces a passive status change as described earlier.
* `role="alert"`
* `role="log"`
* `role="status"`

[How to test in detail for 4.1.3 Status Messages](https://github.com/alphagov/guide-to-wcag/wiki/4.1.3)

## Common mistakes

### Status change that isn't communicated to assistive technologies

<figure>
  <%= image_tag "../images/413/413-fail-event-added.png", { :alt => '"Add event" button with green status message of "Event has been added to calendar"' } %>
</figure>

Code:

```html
<div>Event has been added to calendar</div>
```

This example shows a new status which has been added to the page using a `<div>` tag. However, the element doesn't have a `role`.

This fails because the status is added in a way that doesn't communicate the addition to assistive technologies. It should include an appropriate `role` or `aria-live` attribute. 

### Status change that's communicated with the wrong importance

<figure>
  <%= image_tag "../images/413/413-fail-checks-complete.png", { :alt => 'A tick followed by "System checks complete, no issues found"' } %>
</figure>

Code:

```html
<div id="log" role="alert">System checks complete, no issues found</div>
```

This example shows a confirmation message that has been added to a status log. This element includes `role="alert"`, which makes sure that assistive technologies will be notified about it.

However, this is not advised because the 'upload complete' status is given an unexpectedly high importance ('alert'). This status doesn't require the user's immediate attention, so a more passive `role` like 'status' should be used instead.

Alternatively, instead of adding a `role` attribute to each status, the status log could have `role="log"` added to it. This implicitly adds the `aria-live="polite"` attribute, which announces any changes to the log's content with low importance.

Code:

```html
<div id="log" role="log">System checks complete, no issues found</div>
```

## Good examples

### Status change that's communicated passively

<figure>
  <%= image_tag "../images/413/413-pass-changes-saved.png", { :alt => 'A message of "Your changes have been saved" under a text box containing a passage from Alice in Wonderland' } %>
</figure>

Code:

```html
<div role="status">Your changes have been saved</div>
```

This example shows a new element just added to the page with a `role` of `status`, informing the user that their changes have been saved successfully.

This passes, as it informs assistive technologies of the addition and will not interrupt the user's current task to inform them of the change.

An alternative way to communicate the status would for the `<div>` element to include an `aria-live="polite"` attribute.

### Status change that's communicated urgently

<figure>
  <%= image_tag "../images/413/413-pass-logout-warning.png", { :alt => 'Warning message of "You are about to be logged out" with a timer and options to stay logged in or log out' } %>
</figure>

Code:
 
```html
<div class="logout-warning" role="alert"> 
  <h2>You are about to be logged out</h2> 
  <div>You will be logged out in 23 seconds.</div> 
  <button>Keep me logged in</button> 
  <a href="/logout">Log me out</a> 
</div>
```

This example adds a new element to the page with a `role` of `alert`, informing the user that they are about to be logged out. 

This passes as it informs assistive technologies of the addition. The code interrupts what's currently being read and moves keyboard focus to the warning message. This is fine in this case because the only current action is for the user to extend their login or log out. 

### Progress status of an ongoing task

<figure>
  <%= image_tag "../images/413/413-pass-upload-progress.png", { :alt => 'A blue "upload progress" bar that is half completed.' } %>
</figure>

Code:

```html
<div id="progress-bar" role="progressbar" aria-valuemin="0" aria-valuemax="100" aria-valuenow="50">Upload progress</div>
```

This example defines a 'progress bar' element with a minimum value of 0 and a maximum value of 100. As a file is uploaded, the value of `aria-valuenow` is updated and passively announced to users. The text inside of the element is treated as the accessible label.

This passes as it passively informs assistive technologies of the upload progress and will not interrupt the user's current task with superfluous information.

The HTML `<progress>` element implicitly sets the same role and ARIA attributes and should be used whenever it is appropriate to the situation.

## Related success criteria

This criterion is about how status changes are communicated to assistive technologies. Content that updates live or is dismissed after a period of time must provide users with a mechanism to control when those updates or dismissals take place.

* [1.3.1 Info and Relationships (A)](1.3.1.html) requires that any information presented visually is also available to assistive technologies – without requiring changes to be announced live.
* [2.2.1 Timing Adjustable (A)](2.2.1.html) helps users to control how long content is visible for.
* [4.1.2 Name, Role, Value (A)](4.1.2.html) relates to using code to ensure that user interface components can be read by assistive technologies. But this does not specifically cover live status messages.
